 { In the position the pawn structure speaks clearly in Black's favour - various white pawns are located on light squares, which means that they can be attacked by the black bishop, whereas Black himself has managed to put almost all his pawns on dark squares, out of reach for the enemy bishop. } 1... { -KEY- } g5 $1 ( { A key move, which fixes White's kingside pawns on light squares. Note that with White to move, } 0... g5 0. g5 $1 { would be a great idea, fixing the g-pawns of both sides on the 'right' colour, and at the same time restricting the enemy king. } ) 
2. Kf2 Kf6 3. Ke3 Ke5 4. Be2 Bc2 { Preparing actions on the queenside. } ( 4... Bg2 { is less effective due to } 5. Kf2 { , with the idea } Bxh3 $2 6. Kg3 ) 
5. Kd2 Bb1 6. Ke3 h6 $1 { A useful piece of prophylaxis, placing the last pawn on a dark square, in similar fashion to Example 239 (Aaberg-Tiviakov). } 7. Bf3 ( { Or } 7. Bd1 Be4 $1 { (zugzwang) } 8. Be2 Bc2 { , followed by ...Ba4 - Smyslov, in similar spirit to the game. } ) 
Bc2 8. Be2 a6 9. Kd2 Ba4 10. Ke3 b5 $1 { Black couldn't capitalize on either weakness on c4 and h3, so instead he starts undermining the d5-pawn. } ( { After } 10... b5 { , there is the threat of }  bxc4 12. Bxc4 Bb5 ) 
11. cxb5 axb5 12. Bf3 ( { Or } 12. c4 bxc4 13. Bxc4 Bc2 { , followed by ...Be4 and the d5-pawn falls - Smyslov. } ) 
Bb3 13. Be2 Bc4 $1 14. Bxc4 ( { The ensuing pawn ending is lost; but after the alternative } 14. Bf3 Bxd5 { followed by ... Bc4 and ...d6-d5-d4, Black also wins comfortably. } ) 
bxc4 15. a4 Kxd5 16. a5 Kc6 17. Ke4 d5+ 18. Ke5 d4 $1 { A decisive breakthrough, which must have been anticipated by Smyslov far in advance. } 19. cxd4 c3 20. d5+ Kd7 $1 21. a6 c2 22. a7 c1=Q 23. a8=Q Qf4# 
 0-1

