 { Just like the previous example, the pawn structure determines that Black has the better bishop, though the weakness on d4 in itself won't be sufficient to win the game. } 1... { -KEY- } g5 $1 { With the strong idea of ...g5-g4, fixing the g-pawns on the 'right' colour, before White accomplishes a similar goal by Kf3 and g3-g4. } 2. Kf3 g4+ 3. hxg4+ hxg4+ 4. Ke3 Be7 { The bishop is heading for d6, where it will attack the fresh weakness on g3 and also deny the white king the f4-square, which means that the black king can look for new destinies. } 5. Bc3 Bd6 6. Be1 Ke6 7. Kd3 { Passive defence. } ( { The idea of bringing the bishop to f4 won't work: } 7. Kf2 Bc7 $1 { (zugzwang) } 8. Ke2 ( { or } 8. Bd2 $2 Kf5 { and the king enters at e4 } ) 
Kd7 9. Kf2 Kc6 10. Bd2 Kb5 11. Bf4 Bb6 12. Ke3 Kb4 { etc - Müller. } ) 
Kd7 8. Ke2 Kc6 9. Kd3 Kb5 10. Kc2 a5 $1 { Pawn exchanges usually favour the defender, but there is no rule without exception. Here Kramnik has seen that he needs to swap the queenside pawns in order to progress further with his king. } 11. Kd3 a4 12. bxa4+ ( { Trying to keep the enemy king at bay with } 12. Kc2 { fails to } a3 13. Kb1 Bb4 $1 14. Bxb4 ( { or } 14. Bf2 Bd2 { , followed by Kb4-c3; } ) 
Kxb4 15. Ka2 Kc3 16. Kxa3 Kxd4 17. Kb2 Kd3 18. b4 Kc4 { - Müller. } ) 
Kxa4 13. Bf2 ( { Or } 13. Kc2 Ka3 $1 { (zugzwang) } 14. Bf2 Kb4 15. Kd3 Kb3 { , transposing to the game. } ) 
Kb3 { Despite the reduced material, White is doomed due to his two weaknesses. } 14. Be1 Kb2 15. Bf2 ( { Again White can't prevent the enemy king raid; i.e. } 15. Kd2 $2 Bb4 ) 
Kc1 16. Be3+ Kd1 17. Bf2 Ba3 $1 { Heading for the c1-h6 diagonal in order to leave White without bishop moves. } 18. Ke3 ( { Or } 18. Be3 Ke1 19. Bf4 Bb4 20. Be5 Kf2 { , followed by ...Kf3 and ...Be1, as later occurs in the game. } ) 
Bc1+ 19. Kd3 Bd2 $1 { A nice piece of zugzwang. } 20. Be3 ( 20. Bg1 Be1 21. Bh2 Bf2 { is equally hopeless - Müller. } ) 
Be1 21. Bf4 Bf2 22. Be5 Ke1 23. Kc3 Ke2 24. Kb4 Kf3 25. Kc5 Ke4 $1 { White finally loses one of his pawns, and duly resigned. In Müller's words: 'e4 was a key square right from the start, but who would have thought that Kramnik's king would come from f3 to get there?' } 
 0-1

