 { The idea of fixing pawns remains relevant with rooks accompanying the bishops on the board, as in the next example.A brief look reveals that White has a huge positional advantage, but the weakness on d6 won't be enough by itself for a win. Thus Tiviakov starts actions on the flanks, with the purpose of opening a new front. } 1. { -KEY- } h4 $1 { Preparing h4-h5 in order to fix the h-pawns on the right colour. Moreover, a stronghold on g6 is created for the rooks. } Rc6 2. h5 Rb7 3. Be3 Rb8 ( { Black is limited to passive defence, since } 3... b5 $2 { would run into } 4. cxb5 axb5 5. a5 $1 { creating a powerful passed pawn - Tiviakov. } ) 
4. Bd4 Rc7 5. Rg1 $1 { Heading for g6. } Bg7 ( { Virtually abandoning the h6-pawn; but } 5... Rg7 6. Rg6 $1 Rxg6 7. hxg6 { would just be depressing for Black, who faces the threat of Kg4-h5 and Be3, among other things. } ) 
6. Rg6 Rf7 7. Be3 Rd7 8. Rf5 { White is in no hurry to take the h6-pawn, and first improves his pieces. } ( { The tactical shot } 8. e5 $5 { was interesting as well. } ) 
Rf7 9. Kg4 Bf8 10. Bxh6 Bxh6 11. Rxh6 { Rook endings are known for their drawing potential, but with a sound extra pawn, a powerful passer on h5 and more active pieces, White is clearly winning. } Rg7+ ( { Or } 11... Rg8 12. Rg6 Rxg6 13. hxg6 Rf8 14. a5 $1 { in similar fashion to the game - Tiviakov. } ) 
12. Kf4 Rf8 13. a5 $1 { Opening a new front on the queenside. } Rg1 { Anand sacrifices a pawn for activity. } ( { On } 13... bxa5 { Tiviakov had prepared } 14. e5 dxe5 15. Rxe5 Kd6 16. Rxa5 Ra7 17. Kf5 { etc. } ) 
14. axb6 Rb1 15. b7 Rxb3 16. e5 $1 { This carefully prepared shot decides the battle. } dxe5+ 17. Rxe5+ Kd6 ( { Or } 17... Kf7 18. Rh7 Kg8 19. Ree7 { - Tiviakov. } ) 
18. Rf5 Ke6 ( { Again the seventh rank can't be stepped onto: } 18... Ke7 19. Rh7 Rf7 20. Rxf7 Kxf7 21. Rb5 $1 ) 
19. Rh7 Rd8 ( { Or } 19... Rb8 20. Rc5 $1 Rb6 21. Rcc7 { - Tiviakov. } ) 
20. Rd5 $1 ( { In view of } 20. Rd5 Rxd5 21. cxd5 Kxd5 22. h6 { followed by Rg7 and h6-h7, Black resigned. } ) 

 1-0

