 { The idea of fixing pawns can also occur in knight endings. Here is one example of this.Black is clearly better here, with the more active king and a tremendous knight on c3, which paralyses the opponent's play. But how to make progress? } 1... { -KEY- } b5 $1 { Preparing ...a5-a4 in order to fix a weakness on a3, while depriving White's knight of the c4-square. } 2. Kg2 a4 3. b4 ( { Or } 3. bxa4 bxa4 { with the inevitable threat of }  Nb5 5. Nb1 Kc4 ) 
Ke5 { Amplifying the battle on the kingside. } ( { The tactical shot } 3... Na2 $6 4. Kg3 Nxb4 $4 { would not succeed after } 5. axb4 a3 6. Nb1 $1 { - Ivkov. } ) 
4. Kg3 f5 5. gxf5 ( { Or } 5. g5 f4 $1 6. exf4 Kf5 7. h4 Ne2 8. Kg2 Kxf4 { keeping a huge advantage. } ) 
Kxf5 6. f3 Ke5 { White is starting to run out of solid moves. } 7. Kf2 f5 8. Kg3 $6 { Abandoning the e3-pawn. } ( { a) After } 8. fxe4 $2 Nxe4 9. Ke2 Nxd2 10. Kxd2 Ke4 11. Ke2 h6 12. h3 h5 13. h4 f4 { White is also lost. } ) 
 ( { b) But } 8. f4 { seems a bit more tenacious. One idea for Black here is } Ke6 9. Ke1 Kd5 10. Kf2 Nd1 $1 11. Ke2 Nb2 { (by choosing this route to c4 for his knight, Black doesn't let White's king reach c2) } 12. Kf2 Nc4 13. Nb1 Ke6 { and the transfer of the king to h3 should decide. } ) 
Nd1 $1 9. fxe4 ( { In the event of } 9. Nf1 { one winning line is } Nxe3 $1 10. Nxe3 f4 11. Kf2 fxe3 12. Kxe3 exf3 13. Kxf3 Kd4 ) 
Nxe3 { Clearing the long diagonal for the king. } 10. exf5 Nxf5+ 11. Kf3 Kd4 $1 12. Kf4 Nd6 ( { Preparing } 12... Nd6  Nc4 { , while }  Kc3 { is another dreadful threat. } ) 

