 { Let's see another knight ending, this time with rooks on the board.Interestingly, both sides have difficulties in realizing their respective pawn majority. Smyslov found a clever idea. } 1. { -KEY- } a5 $1 { Preparing a5-a6 to fix a weakness on a7. The obvious Ne5-c6 can wait until a more suitable moment. } Nc5 { The most solid reply. } ( { a) Both } 1... bxa5 $6 2. Ra1 ) 
 ( { b) And } 1... Rd2 $6 2. c5 $1 { are unpleasant for Black; in this line there can follow } bxc5 3. Rxc5 Nxc5 4. Kxd2 Nb3 5. Kd3 Nxa5 6. Nc6 $1 { and the b-pawn soon becomes a telling factor. } ) 
2. a6 { From now on, the a7-pawn will be a real headache for Black. } ( { A different, interesting idea was } 2. axb6 axb6 3. Ra1 { intending to invade with the rook along the seventh rank. } ) 
Kg7 3. Rc2 $1 { Preparing Rd2 in order to exchange the main defender of the a7-pawn, something that Black can't allow to happen. } Ne4 4. g4 $1 ( { Seeing that } 4. Nc6 Rd7 { is unconstructive, Smyslov grabs some space on the opposite flank. Meanwhile, Black can only wait. } ) 
Kf6 5. g5+ Kg7 ( { Obviously, } 5... Nxg5 $2 { failed to } 6. Nc6 ) 
6. Nc6 Rd7 7. Rg2 $1 ( { a) White now aims at opening a new front on the kingside by h2-h4-h5; but first rules out } 7. Rg2 h5 $2 { on account of } 8. gxh6 Kxh6 9. Rxg6 $1 ) 
 ( { b) In contrast, the combination } 7. c5 Nxc5 8. Rxc5 $6 bxc5 9. b6 axb6 10. a7 Rxa7 11. Nxa7 { would permit } h6 $1 12. h4 hxg5 13. hxg5 e5 $1 14. fxe5 Kf7 { with unclear play - Smyslov. } ) 
Kf8 8. h4 Ke8 9. Rh2 Kf8 10. h5 { Getting on with the plan. } ( { The } 10. c5 $5 { breakthrough was also interesting; e.g. } Nxc5 ( { or } 10. h5 bxc5 11. b6 axb6 12. a7 ) 
11. Rd2 $1 Rc7 12. Rd8 Kg7 13. Ra8 Nxa6 14. bxa6 Rxc6 15. Rxa7 Kg8 16. Rb7 { , followed by a6-a7, when Black loses his rook due to the badly placed king, in similar fashion to Example 201 (Van Wely-Giri). } ) 
gxh5 ( { After } 10... Kg7 $6 11. hxg6 hxg6 12. Rh6  { and } 13. Ne5 { , the g6-pawn is doomed. } ) 
11. Rxh5 Kg8 12. Rh1 ( { Seeing that the immediate } 12. Rh6 { can be met by } Nc5 { Smyslov looks for a zugzwang. } ) 
Kg7 13. Ne5 Rd8 14. Rh6 $1 { Just at the right moment. Now Black's defensive resources are soon exhausted. } Rd6 ( 14... Nc5 { was even worse due to } 15. Rf6 $1 { and the rook enters at f7 - Smyslov, now that } Rf8 { fails to } 16. Rxf8 Kxf8 17. Nc6 ) 
15. Ke2 $1 ( { A technical choice; avoiding even the slightest enemy counterplay after } 15. Nc6 $6 Nd2 ) 
Nc3+ ( { Or } 15... Rd2 16. Ke1 Rd6 17. Nc6 { with a quick win - Smyslov. } ) 
16. Ke1 Rd1+ 17. Kf2 Rd4 18. Kf3 { Now Black's pieces have definitely lost track, and the rest is easy. } Rd6 19. Rf6 $1 Kg8 20. Rf7 Rd3+ 21. Nxd3 ( { In view of } 21. Nxd3 Kxf7 22. Ne5 { followed by Nc6xa7, Black resigned. } ) 

