 { In this position, all White's pawns are on the 'wrong' colour, but this is hardly a big deal since only the one on d5 cannot move on to a dark square. On the other hand, White's space advantage and more active king are concerning factors for Black. } 1. { -KEY- } h4 $1 { In order to fix the g6-pawn on a light square, before Black plays ...g6-g5 himself. } ( { Of course the immediate } 1. g5 $2 { failed to } Bxh3 ) 
h6 ( { a) Unfortunately for Black, the 'active' } 1... Kf6 $6 2. g5 Ke5 $4 { runs into } 3. Ke3 { with mate, so his king has to stay back. } ) 
 ( { b) However, } 1... b4 $5 { fixing pawns on the 'right' colour, was worth considering, when Black would avoid the weakness on b5 that soon emerges in the game; e.g. } 2. Kc4 a5 3. g5 h6 { followed by ...hxg5 and ...Kf7, when I see no obvious winning plan for White. } ) 
2. g5 hxg5 3. hxg5 Kf7 4. a3 $1 { Just like Smyslov's 6...h6 against Tsvetkov (inExample 256), White puts a pawn on the right colour in advance. } a5 ( { The attempt to gain some space with } 4... e5 $2 { fails to } 5. dxe6 Bxe6 6. Kc3 { and both the white king and bishop are ready to attack the queenside pawns. } ) 
5. Kd4 ( { There is no hurry with } 5. b4 { since after } 5. Kd4 { Black can't play } b4 $2 { anyway: } 6. axb4 axb4 7. Kc4 ) 
Kg7 6. Bd3 Kf7 7. f4 Kg7 8. Kc3 Kf7 9. b4 $1 ( { Only now does Polgar fix the queenside; when Black can't swap pawns with } 9. b4 axb4 $2 { due to } 10. Kxb4 ) 
a4 10. Kd4 { Compared with the initial position White has made considerable progress - two weaknesses have been fixed: on g6 and b5; besides, there is the idea of advancing f4-f5 at the right moment, creating an outside passed pawn. } Kg7 11. Ke3 Kf7 12. Kf3 Kg7 13. Kg3 Kf7 14. Be2 Kg7 ( 14... e5 { wouldn't really help Black's case after } 15. dxe6 $1 Kxe6 16. Bd3 { followed by a king transfer to d4, e.g. } Be8 17. Kf3 Kd5 18. Ke3 { (zugzwang) } Ke6 19. Kd4 Ke7 20. Kd5 { (again zugzwang) } Bf7 21. Kc6 Be8 22. Kb6 { . In this variation, we can see that the departure of the d5-pawn just helps White's king in the end. } ) 
15. Bg4 $5 Bf5 $1 ( { a) Not } 15... Bxg4 $2 16. Kxg4 Kf7 17. f5 { , when the outside passed pawn on g5 soon decides matters. } ) 
 ( { b) Whereas, after } 15... Bf5 16. Bxf5 $2 gxf5 17. Kh4 Kg6 { leads to a draw, so White adjusts her plans. } ) 
16. Be2 Bd7 17. Bf1 Kf7 ( { Avoiding } 17... Be8 18. Bh3 { when Black's bishop is dominated by its counterpart, although Polgar soon finds how to force this situation anyway. } ) 
18. Bd3 Kg7 19. Kh4 $1 Be8 ( { On } 19... Kf7 { there follows the same reply. } ) 
20. Be2 Kf8 ( { It turns out that, with the white king on h4 , } 20... Bd7 21. Bg4 Bf5 { no longer works, due to } 22. Bxf5 gxf5 23. Kh5 ) 
21. Bg4 Bf7 22. Be6 $1 { A strong reply, avoiding any black counterplay. This would have followed on 21... Kf8 as well. } Be8 ( { After } 22... Bxe6 23. dxe6 { White wins by bringing her king to c6 - De Dovitiis. } ) 
23. Kg4 { After seizing the h3-c8 diagonal, the next step in White's plan is Bc8-b7-c6, but first it makes sense to return with the king to the centre. Meanwhile, Black is confined to passive defence. } Kg7 24. Kf3 Kf8 25. Ke4 Kg7 26. Bc8 Kf7 27. Kd4 Kf8 28. Bb7 Bd7 29. Bc6 $1 Ke8 ( { Or } 29... Bxc6 30. dxc6 Ke8 31. f5 { and the separated passed pawns take their toll. } ) 
30. f5 $1 { A nice final touch. } ( { In view of } 30. f5 gxf5 31. g6 { Black resigned. } ) 

 1-0

