 { In bishop vs. knight endings, the idea of fixing pawns is a frequent idea for both sides. In his ChessBase comments to the game Sokolov-Sutovsky examined later on in this section, Krasenkow even argued that: 'Fixing your rival's pawns on the squares of your bishop, after which they become weak - this is the main strategic principle in the bishop vs. knight endgame!' Let's see some examples.Such positions with mutual passed pawns are clearly in the bishop's favour, though precision is still required. } 1... { -KEY- } a5 $1 { Fixing the a4-pawn as a future target, and advancing the potential passed pawn as well. } ( { Obviously, } 1... Bb3 $6 2. a5 { would be less to the point. } ) 
2. Kf4 ( { Or } 2. Kh6 Bd5 3. Nf6 Bf3 4. Kxh7 Kxc5 5. Kg6 Kb4 6. Kg5 Kxa4 7. Nxg4 Bxg4 $1 8. Kxg4 Kb4 { and wins - Speelman. } ) 
Bb3 $2 ( 2... h5 $1 { first was more precise, so that White's king transfer to the queenside with } 3. Ke3 { can be met by } h4 $1 { intending a breakthrough with }  g3 5. hxg3 h3 { . } ) 
3. Ke3 ( { After } 3. Kxg4 Bxa4 4. Kg5 Bc2 { Black wins even more comfortably. } ) 
Bxa4 4. Kd2 ( { Seeing that } 4. Nf6 { fails to } Bc2 { White denies the bishop that square. } ) 
h6 $1 { A useful move, in order to delay White's conquest of that pawn. } 5. Nf6 Kxc5 6. Nxg4 h5 7. Ne3 ( { A surprising way to the draw was } 7. Nf6 h4 8. Ng4 $1 ( { not } 8. Ne4 $2 Kd4 9. Nd6 Bd7 $1 { and the knight is fatally marginated from the battle - Speelman. } ) 
Kd4 9. Ne3 { when Black can't progress properly without letting White sacrifice the knight on the h-pawn. } ) 
Kd4 8. Nf1 $2 ( { The decisive mistake. White should have played } 8. h4 $1 { instead, in the spirit of our main theme, albeit in a slightly different context. By fixing the h5-pawn White gets ready to sacrifice the knight on it, either by f5-g3 o f1-g3, after which Black is sitting with the a-pawn and the wrong bishop, as observed by Farciarz. } ) 
Ke5 9. Ke3 Bb3 { Here White resigned. Let's see a possible conclusion: } 10. Ng3 a4 11. Kd3 a3 12. Kc3 a2 13. Kb2 h4 14. Ne2 Ke4 { Black wins - Speelman. } 
 0-1

