 { White's activity is overwhelming, but in order to progress he needs a new weakness to work on. } 1. { -KEY- } h4 $1 { Fixing the h-pawns on squares of the 'right' colour. } gxh4 2. gxh4 Be4 { The only move, so as to prevent Ng2-f4. Nevertheless, by clever knight manoeuvres White soon forces the arrival of that piece on f4 anyway. } 3. Nf1 ( { Even faster was } 3. Nd1 Bd3 4. Nf2 Bf5 5. a3 $1 { followed by either 6.Nd3 or 6.Nh3 next move - 'EDAMI'. } ) 
Bf3 4. Nd2 Be2 ( { After the alternative } 4... Bg2 5. Nb3 Bf3 6. Nd4 Bg4 { White can simply throw in } 7. a3 { losing a tempo, with similar play as in the game. } ) 
5. Nb3 Bg4 ( { Another, perhaps not-so-obvious asset for White during this whole example is his reserve of waiting moves: a2-a3, b2-b3 and b3-b4, whereas Black doesn't have even one. Thus after } 5... Bd1 6. Nd4 Bg4 { White can lose a tempo by } 7. a3 $1 { again with similar consequences to the game. } ) 
6. Nd4 $1 { Zugzwang: since the king is busy opposing its white counterpart, Black finally has to leave the d1-h5 diagonal with his bishop. } Bh3 7. Ne2 Bf5 8. Nf4 Bg4 9. b4 $1 { Again Black is in zugzwang, and now he must give up a pawn. } Kd7 10. Kb6 Bf3 11. Kxa6 Kc6 12. Nxe6 
