 { Black has just played ...c6-c5 with the obvious intention of ...d5-d4+, after which his bishop would become great on c6. However, any such aspirations are effectively crushed by Alekhine's next, strong move. } 1. { -KEY- } d4 $1 { Fixing the d5-pawn as a future attacking object, while placing his own d-pawn on a dark square, out of reach for the enemy bishop. The fact that Black now obtains a protected passed pawn is irrelevant. } c4 2. f5 $1 ( { A nice follow-up which enables } 3. Nf4 { , an idea that Yates hurries to prevent. } ) 
g5 3. h4 $1 { In the same spirit as two moves ago, fixing a weakness on h5 while softening up the dark squares on the kingside. } f6 4. hxg5 { Alekhine hurries to create a powerful pair of connected passed pawns. } ( { The more restrained } 4. Ng1 { would allow } gxh4 5. gxh4 Bd7 6. e6 Bc6 7. Ne2 Kd6 8. Nf4 Be8 { with some chances for a draw. } ) 
fxg5 5. Ng1 $1 { Heading for f3 or h3 in order to soften up the dark squares even more. } Bd7 $2 ( { The active } 5... h4 { was called for, although after } 6. g4 Ba4 7. Ke2 $5 { preparing Nh3, White keeps good winning chances. } ) 
6. f6+ Ke8 ( { Or } 6... Ke6 $2 7. Nh3 { winning - Primel. } ) 
7. Nf3 { Now it turns out that Black has no other choice than to put one more pawn on a light square, and the rest is child's play for Alekhine. } g4 8. Nh4 Be6 9. Ng6 $1 { The knight finally reaches its desired destiny at f4. } Bf7 10. Nf4 Kd7 11. Ke2 $1 { Zugzwang. } a5 12. Ke3 Bg8 13. Nxh5 Bf7 14. Nf4 Bg8 15. Ne2 { It is the king's turn to use the f4-square, in order to invade the enemy camp. Yates fights on until the bitter end. } Be6 16. Kf4 Ke8 17. Kg5 Kf7 18. Nc3 Kf8 19. Kg6 Kg8 20. f7+ $1 Kf8 21. Kf6 Bxf7 22. e6 Bh5 23. Nxd5 Be8 24. Nc3 { In view of the imminent advance of the d-pawn, Black resigned. } 
 1-0

