 { Here is a similar example, where the presence of rooks doesn't really help the defender a lot.A nightmare position for White: his bishop has no scope at all, and the pawns on b3 and e4 are easy targets for Black's pieces. With his next move, Yandemirov increased his advantage. } 1... { -KEY- } g5 $1 { Fixing another weakness on g4, while ruling out any ideas of g4-g5 and f5-f6. } 2. Kg3 ( { Or } 2. fxg6 fxg6 3. Kg3 g5 $1 { with similar play. } ) 
Kg7 3. hxg5 hxg5 4. Rd1 Na6 { Despite b4 being a protected square, the black knight wasn't really doing anything there, and is now redirected to the more useful c5-square. } 5. Re1 ( { More stubborn was } 5. Ra1 Nc5 6. Ra3 { although after } ( { not } 6. Rxa5 $2 Nxe4 ) 
Kf6 { (there is no rush to take the pawn) } 7. Bf3 Nxe4 8. Bxe4 Rxe4 9. Rxa5 Re3 10. Kf2 Rxb3 { Black should win. Even so, rook endings always leave some additional hope for the defender. } ) 
Nc5 6. Re3 Re8 $1 7. Kf2 { Forgetting about the b3-pawn. } ( { Instead, after the more solid } 7. Bf3 { Black would prevail by } Kf6 { bringing up the king - Atlas; e.g. } 8. Kf2 Ke5 { with ...Rb8 and ...Kd4 next, when White inevitably loses material. } ) 
Rb8 8. e5 dxe5 
