 { Usually the opponent's pawn is fixed on the colour of the bishop, as in the previous examples, but not always. Here are two such exceptions.In such blocked positions the knight tends to be stronger than the bishop, and here White is favoured by the fact that several pawn pairs are fixed on the 'right' colour, which means that the white pawns cannot be attacked by the bishop. But how to progress? } 1. { -KEY- } a6 $1 { A move with several virtues: a weakness is fixed on a7, a tempo is gained for a future passed pawn race, and the enemy bishop is tied to the defence of the b5-pawn, now that ...a7-a6 will no longer be possible. } ( { A different move order with } 1. fxe4 { first and only then 2.a6 also works, as observed by Neilthesculptor. } ) 
g6 ( { If } 1... exf3 2. gxf3 g6 { to avoid the zugzwang that follows in the game, White has the strong } 3. e4 $1 { ; e.g. } fxe4 4. fxe4 dxe4 5. Nxe4 Ke6 6. Nc3 { and the fresh passed pawn takes its toll after } Kf6 7. d5 Bd3 8. Ne4 $1 Bxe4 ( { or } 8... Ke7 9. Ke5 ) 
9. Kxe4 g5 10. hxg5 Kxg5 11. Ke5 $1 Kg6 12. Ke6 h4 13. d6 { winning Black's queen in the end - Smyslov. } ) 
2. fxe4 fxe4 ( { Or } 2... dxe4 3. g3 { followed by a timely d5-d6; e.g. } Bd3 4. d5 Bc4 5. d6 $1 Ke6 6. Kg5 Kxd6 7. Kxg6 Ke5 8. Kxh5 Kf6 9. Kh6 Bd3 10. Nd5 Ke5 11. Kg7 $1 Kxd5 12. h5 { and wins - Smyslov. } ) 
3. g3 $1 { Zugzwang - it is easy to see that Black has no solid move at his disposal. } Ke6 4. Kg5 Kf7 5. Nd1 $1 { The knight's ability to touch almost any square on the board means that it should usually move around a lot in the endgame. } Bf1 6. Nf2 Kg7 7. g4 $1 { The pawn helps the knight enter Black's camp. } hxg4 ( { Or } 7... Be2 8. gxh5 gxh5 9. Nh3 { heading for f4. } ) 
8. Nxg4 Bh3 9. Nf6 Be6 10. Ne8+ ( { There follows } 10. Ne8+  11. Nc7 { or } 11. Nd6 { with devastating effect, so Black resigned. } ) 

 1-0

