 { This position with an open centre is clearly to the bishop's liking, though Black has achieved a barrier which prevents White's king from invading the enemy camp. With his next, strong move Benko addresses the issue. } 1. { -KEY- } h5 $1 { White deliberately fixes the h-pawns on squares of the 'wrong' colour in order to soften up the f5-square for the king. } ( { The stereotypical } 1. g5 $6 { would achieve less after } hxg5 $1 2. hxg5 Ke7 { intending }  fxg5 4. fxg5 Ne6 { with excellent drawing chances - Shereshevsky. } ) 
g5 ( { a) The active } 1... f5 { backfires after } 2. Ke5 $1 ) 
 ( { b) While } 1... gxh5 2. gxh5 { followed by Kf5, soon forces Black to let the king enter at e6; } ) 
 ( { c) And } 1... Kg7 2. hxg6 Kxg6 3. Bd3 { is similar. } ) 
2. fxg5 fxg5 3. Kf5 Kg7 4. Ke5 $1 { A typical ploy: having diverted the enemy king, White directs his own king to the opposite flank. } Ne8 5. Be6 Nf6 6. b4 $1 { Before invading with the king it makes sense to advance the queenside pawns, anticipating a potential passed pawn race. } Ne8 7. b5 Nf6 ( { Or } 7... Kf8 8. Bd7 { and the king enters at d6 anyway. } ) 
8. a4 Ne8 9. Bf5 Nf6 10. Ke6 Ne8 11. Be4 ( { Another good option was } 11. Ke7 { and the king accesses d6 on the next move. } ) 
Nf6 12. Bf3 Ng8 13. Kd6 Kf8 ( { Or } 13... Kf6 14. Bd5 Ne7 15. Be6 $1 { trapping the knight in curious fashion. } ) 
14. Kc6 Ke7 15. Kb7 Kd6 16. Kxa7 Kc5 17. Ka6 ( { Avoiding } 17. a5 bxa5 18. b6 Nf6 { followed by ...Nd7, when White would have to find } 19. b7 Nd7 20. Bd1 $1 { preventing ...a5-a4 while getting ready for Ba4 or Bc2-f5. } ) 
Nf6 18. Bc6 $1 { The magic distance, preventing Black's knight from assisting in the queenside defence. } Nxg4 19. a5 bxa5 20. b6 Ne5 21. Be8 
 1-0

