 ( { The open centre and the existence of pawns on both flanks evidently favour the bishop. Instead of the routine } 1. Be8 Ke7 { , Carlsen found a more long-term plan. } ) 
 { -KEY- } g5 $1 { Realizing the idea of fixing enemy pawns in a slightly different fashion than in the previous examples. Here White's h-pawn is not fixed at h3, but a later attack with the bishop will force it to move forward, and after ...g5xh4, g3xh4, White's split kingside pawns will prove more vulnerable to Black's king. } ( { a) Less promising was } 1... Ke7 2. Ke2 Ke6 3. Nc3 $1 Ke5 ( { or } 3... b2 4. Kd3 { followed by Kc2; } ) 
4. Kd3 $5 Ba6 5. Ke3 { with f2-f4 next } ) 
 ( { b) Or } 1... Bf3 2. Nb2 Ke7 3. Ke1 Ke6 4. Kd2 Kd5 5. Kc3 Kxc5 6. Kxb3 Kd4 7. Kc2 { when the battle on one single flank is to the knight's taste. } ) 
2. Ke2 Bg2 3. h4 gxh4 4. gxh4 Kg7 ( { Immediately heading for the kingside pawns; although } 4... Ke7 5. Kd3 Bh3 { was worth considering too - Baburin. } ) 
5. Kd3 Kg6 6. Kc4 Kh5 7. Kxb3 Kxh4 { Black has obtained the sort of position he was looking for, with mutual passed pawns, where the bishop tends to outperform the knight. } 8. Ne3 Bb7 9. Kc4 h5 10. Kd4 $2 ( { The active } 10. Kb5 $1 { preparing c5-c6, was called for; e.g. } Kg5 11. c6 Bc8 12. Kc5 h4 13. Nf1 $1 Kg4 14. Nh2 Kh3 15. Nf3 $1 { and Black cannot make progress, since } Bg4 { is met by } 16. c7 { with a draw - Baburin. Of course such decisions are more difficult at the board, so you could say that, although 1...g5 might not win theoretically, it does make White's task a bit harder in practice. } ) 
Kg5 11. Ke5 h4 12. f4+ Kg6 13. Ng4 { White hurries to block the h-pawn on a dark square. } h3 14. Nh2 f6+ 15. Ke6 Bg2 16. Ng4 Kh5 ( { Correct was } 16... f5 17. Nh2 Be4 $1 { preparing ...Kh5-h4 - Baburin; e.g. } 18. Ke5 Kh5 19. c6 Bxc6 20. Kxf5 Kh4 21. Ke5 Kg3 22. Nf1 Kg2 23. Ne3 Kg1 24. Ng4 Bf3 $1 { and wins. } ) 
17. Nf2 $2 ( { Here } 17. Nh2 $1 { was more tenacious, when Black would have to repeat moves with } Kg6 ( { the tempting } 17. Nf2 Kh4 $2 { only draws after } 18. Kxf6 Kg3 19. f5 Kxh2 20. Ke5 { - Baburin } ) 
 { and if } 18. Ng4 { then } f5 { transposing to the previous note. } ) 
h2 18. Kxf6 Kh4 19. Ke5 Kg3 
