 { A quick glance reveals that Black will soon recover the pawn on d6, but in the process he will have to offer a rook exchange. Considering the potential bishop vs. knight ending, Dreev hurries to fix the kingside pawns on squares of the 'right' colour. } 1. { -KEY- } h4 $1 { Creating a long-term target for the bishop on g6. } Ke6 2. f3 Nd7 ( { Black is forced to spend a few vital tempi on the defence of the g6-pawn, since the straightforward } 2... Rxd6 $2 { fails to } 3. Rxd6 Kxd6 4. Be8 $1 { - Hazai. } ) 
3. Bb5 ( { Removing the bishop from the knight's range, in "magic distance" fashion. In contrast, } 3. Kf2 { allows } Ne5 { with a double attack at c6 and d6. } ) 
Nf8 4. d7 $1 { By handing over the pawn in this way, White makes his opponent waste more time in untangling. } Nxd7 5. Kf2 Ke7 6. Ke3 Nf8 7. Rxd8 $1 Kxd8 8. Ke4 { Dreev has reached his goal: a highly favourable bishop vs. knight ending. } Ne6 9. Ke5 Ke7 10. Bc4 $1 { Before moving his king over to the queenside, White rules out ...Nf4. } Nd8 11. Kd5 ( { Black resigned. An apparently early decision, but } 11. Kd5 Kf6 ( { and } 11. Kd5 Ne6 12. Kc6 ) 
 ( { while after } 11. Kd5 Kd7 12. Bd3 Ne6 13. Ke5 $1 Nf8 14. Kf6 { the story is repeated on the opposite flank; } ) 
12. Kd6 { allow the white king to invade decisively on the queenside - Hazai. } ) 

