 { When there are opposite-coloured bishops on the board, one single weakness or asset often isn't enough for victory. In the position above, White needs to open a new front alongside with his passed b-pawn in order to exhaust the enemy defences. } 1. { -KEY- } h5 $1 { Before activating his king, Cifuentes fixes a long-term weakness on h6. } ( { Less promising was } 1. hxg5 $2 hxg5 2. Kh3 Kd6 $1 3. Bxe4 Kc7 ) 
 ( { Or } 1. Kh3 Kd6 $1 2. Kg4 Kc7 3. h5 Bxe3 4. Bxe4 Bd2 $1 5. Kf5 g4 { in both cases with a draw - Cifuentes. } ) 
Kd5 $6 ( { I think that } 1... g4 $1 { was called for, fixing a weakness on g3 which can be attacked by the bishop on the same diagonal as it controls the b7-pawn; e.g. } 2. Kf2 Bb8 3. Be8 Ke6 4. Bc6 Kf5 5. Bd7 Kg5 6. Be8 Kf5 { and it is not clear how to progress with White. } ) 
2. g4 $1 { Thus White doesn't just secure his g-pawn (cf. 1...g4 above), he also makes the h6-pawn very difficult to defend. In the words of Cifuentes: supposing that White manages to place his king on g6 and the bishop on the a2-g8 diagonal, then the h-pawn would be unstoppable. } Ke5 3. Kf2 { The white king finally starts its march towards the b-pawn. } Bb8 4. Ke2 Ba7 5. Kd2 Kd5 6. Kc3 Bb8 7. Bf7+ Kc5 8. Bg6 Kd5 9. Kb4 Bg3 10. Kb5 Bc7 11. Ka6 $1 ( { By means of the threat } 12. Ka7 { White gains access to the b6-square. By the way, king movements on squares of the 'enemy' colour often play a key role in endings with opposite-coloured bishops, as we already saw inExample 241 (Aronian-Bacrot; 4...Kd7), among others. } ) 
Bb8 12. Kb6 Ke5 13. Kc6 Ke6 { In order to prevent 14.Kd7, Black has to abandon the e4-pawn. } 14. Bxe4 Bg3 15. Bf5+ Ke7 16. Kb6 Bb8 17. e4 Kd6 18. e5+ $1 Ke7 ( { a) Evidently, } 18... Kxe5 19. Kc6 ) 
 ( { b) Or } 18... Kd5 19. Bc8 Kxe5 20. Kc6 Kf6 21. Kd7 Kf7 22. Kd8 { followed by Bf5 and Kc8, leads to a quicker win - Cifuentes. } ) 
19. Bc2 $1 { Regrouping the bishop to the a2-g8 diagonal, so that it won't obstruct a later king transfer via e4-f5. } Ke6 20. Bb3+ Ke7 ( { a) Again } 20... Kxe5 { permits } 21. Kc6 ) 
 ( { b) While after } 20... Kd7 21. Kc5 Bxe5 22. Kd5 { Black has no satisfactory defence against the threat of Ke4-f5-g6. } ) 
21. Ba2 $1 { Zugzwang. } ( { Of course } 21. Kc6 $2 Bxe5 22. Kd5 Kf6 { had to be avoided - Cifuentes. } ) 
Kd7 22. Kc5 $1 { Finally, the invasion with the king via f5-g6 can't be avoided, and the rest is easy. } Bxe5 23. Kd5 Bf4 24. Ke4 Ke7 ( { Or } 24... Kc6 25. Kf5 Kxb7 26. Kg6 Kc6 27. Kxh6 Kd6 28. Kg7 { etc - Cifuentes. } ) 
25. Kf5 Bc7 26. Kg6 
 1-0

