 { Here is one more opposite-coloured bishop ending with an extra pawn. Black has just played ...Ba3-d6, trying to provoke a concession on the kingside. } 1. { -KEY- } g3 $2 { A bad mistake, helping the opponent to fix the h2-pawn as a future target. } ( { After } 1. h3 $1 gxh3 2. gxh3 Ba3 3. Bc6 { I see no winning plan for Black, whose e- and f-pawns can be attacked opportunely by the white bishop. } ) 
Ba3 { Immobilizing the enemy pawn majority. Meanwhile, White can only wait. } 2. Kf2 h5 { Black's plan is evident: ...h5-h4-h3 to fix the h2-pawn and create queening scenarios on the h-file. } 3. Be8 Kg5 4. Bf7 e5 5. Bd5 h4 6. Ke2 ( { After } 6. gxh4 $6 Kxh4 7. Be6 ( { or } 7. Kg2 Bc1 ) 
f4 8. Bf5 Kh3 { Black wins with less effort. } ) 
h3 7. Bc6 Bc5 8. a4 f4 $1 { Creating a pair of connected passed pawns, and clearing the way for the bishop towards g1. } 9. Bxe4 $2 { This natural move turns out to be a losing mistake. } ( { Instead, active defence with } 9. b4 $1 { was called for. After } Bxe3 ( { or } 9. Bxe4 Bxb4 10. Bxe4 f3 11. Kf2 { securing the e3-pawn } ) 
10. a5 bxa5 11. bxa5 Ba7 12. Bxe4 f3 13. Kd3 { White holds. Contrary to the game, here Black's bishop will be busy controlling the a-pawn, so there is no need for the white king to retreat to f1 in order to prevent ...Bg1. On d3, it will instead help keeping Black's king away. } ) 
 ( { Another drawing possibility was } 9. exf4 exf4 10. Bxe4 { . My opponent must have rejected this due to } Bg1 { , however after } 11. gxf4 Kxf4 12. Bc6 Bxh2 13. Kf1 { it turns out that White would soon liquidate the queenside pawns by b3-b4 and a4-a5, and then keep the bishop on the h1-a8 diagonal, where it would be ready to sacrifice itself on g2 for Black´s remaining pawns. If Black brings the king to g3, intending ...Kh2, then White could tie the black king to the defence of the g4-pawn by moving the bishop to the h3-c8 diagonal.A sample variation: } Kg5 14. b4 Bc7 15. a5 $1 bxa5 16. bxa5 Bxa5 17. Bb7 Kh4 18. Bc6 Kg3 19. Bd7 $1 { and Black can't progress. However, it should be noticed that this drawing mechanism only works when one of the pawns is a rook pawn - else the attacker's king could assist the pawns decisively from the side, in this case from a fictive 'i-file' (...Kh4, ...g4-g3, ...Ki3-h2, so to speak). } ) 
Bxe3 10. Kf1 Bc5 11. Bc6 f3 12. Be4 { White is pinning his hopes on a light-square blockade, but it transpires that Black can make a turn with his king without bothering about the g4- and h3-pawns. } Kf6 $1 13. Bb7 Ke6 14. Be4 ( { More tenacious was } 14. Bc8 Kd6 $1 ( { less clear is } 14. Be4 Kd5 $6 15. Bxg4 e4 16. Bc8 $1 { followed by 17.Bb7, indirectly controlling the enemy passed pawns. } ) 
15. Bxg4 ( 15. Bb7 { leads to the game; } ) 
e4 16. Ke1 ( { not } 16. Bxh3 $2 e3 ) 
Ke5 17. Bxh3 e3 18. Bf1 Kd4 { should be winning for Black. One plan at this point is to control the kingside pawns with the bishop, attack the queenside pawns with the king, and finally transpose to a bishop vs. pawns ending by ...e3-e2. } ) 
Kd6 15. Bb7 ( { After } 15. Bf5 Bb4 ( { again } 15. Bb7 Kd5 $6 16. Bxg4 e4 17. Bc8 $1 { followed by 18.Bb7, is less convincing; } ) 
16. Bxg4 e4 17. Kf2 Bc5 18. Ke1 Ke5 { we have transposed to the previous note. } ) 
Bb4 16. Be4 Kc5 { The king finally enters, and the rest is easy. } 17. Bf5 Kd4 18. Bxg4 e4 19. Kf2 Kd3 20. Bf5 Bc5+ 21. Kf1 Kd4 22. Bg4 Kc3 $1 { White is unable to defend both flanks. } 23. Bxh3 e3 24. Bg4 e2+ 25. Ke1 Kd3 ( { In view of } 25... Kd3 26. Bxf3 Bb4 { White resigned. } ) 

