 { When one side possesses the bishop pair in the endgame, the idea of fixing enemy pawns as targets for the bishops is seen rather frequently. Here are a few examples.Thanks to his bishop pair White has a slight but enduring edge. Gurevich treated the endgame in exemplary fashion. } 1. { -KEY- } b5 $1 { Fixing a weakness on a7 for the dark-squared bishop. } f6 ( { Preventing } 1... f6 1. Be5 { and preparing the co-ordination of the pieces by ...Kf7, ...e6-e5 and ...Be6. } ) 
2. Bc3 { The bishop gets ready to switch diagonals when necessary. } Kf7 3. Bc4 ( { The straightforward } 3. Bb4 { can be met by } e5 4. Bd6 Be6 5. Bb8 Nc8 ) 
Ng6 4. Bb4 Ne5 5. Be2 Bb7 { The bishop will prove rather passive here after White's next. } ( { Perhaps } 5... Nd7 { preparing ...Nc5 and ...Bd7, was more promising; e.g. } 6. Bd6 Nc5 7. f3 Bd7 8. Bb8 Na4 $1 9. Bxa7 Nc3 10. Bf1 Nxb5 { with favourable simplifications. } ) 
6. f3 Nd7 7. Bd6 $1 { Tying the knight to the defence of the b8-square. } g6 8. Kf2 f5 { Admittedly, this advance opens up the position for the bishop pair, but Black had no other chance for counterplay. } 9. exf5 gxf5 ( { Or } 9... exf5 10. Bc4 Kf6 11. Ke3 { with a clear advantage - Gurevich. } ) 

