 { Just as in the previous example, the bishops promises a slight but enduring edge. } 1... { -KEY- } g5 $1 { A move with several virtues: Black gains space, mobilizes his pawn majority, and enables ...Kg7-f6, overcoming the barrier of White's minor pieces. Moreover, White is not allowed to set up an ideal kingside structure with h2-h4, which means that his h-pawn can later become fixed on h2 or h3. } ( { For the same reason, } 1... h5 $6 2. h4 $1 { would be less precise. } ) 
2. b4 Bc7 ( { Of course Black isn't tempted by } 2... Bxc6 $2 3. Bxc6 axb4 4. cxb4 Bxb4 5. Bd7 f4 6. Bf5 { when White holds in the opposite-coloured bishop ending despite the pawn minus - Baburin. } ) 
3. f3 Kg7 4. Kf2 Kf6 5. Ke3 h5 $1 { Gaining more space, and preparing to fix a weakness on h2 by ...h5-h4-h3. } 6. Kd3 h4 7. Ke2 ( { It is not easy to give White good advice; for example, } 7. gxh4 $6 gxh4 { would just yield Black additional ideas such as ...e5-e4+ and ... Kg5-f4. } ) 
h3 $1 { Fixing the h2-pawn and creating queening scenarios. } 8. Kf2 Be6 $1 { A well-known merit of the bishop pair is that one of the bishops can be exchanged at some moment in order to achieve another advantage. Here such an exchange helps Black to clear the way for his king towards the enemy queenside, while the potential attacker of the h2-pawn - the dark-squared bishop - remains on the board. } 9. Bxe6 ( { White is also in big trouble after } 9. c4 Bxd5 10. cxd5 axb4 11. Nxb4 Bd6 { , with ideas like ...Bc5+ and ... e5-e4 followed by ...Ke5. } ) 
Kxe6 10. Ke3 ( { Or } 10. b5 Kd5 11. Ne7 Kc4 12. Nxf5 Kb3 $1 { and the a-pawn soon takes its toll. } ) 
b5 $1 { A strong breakthrough that leads to a position with mutual passed pawns, where the bishop tends to outperform the knight. } ( { Another option was } 10... Kd7 11. b5 e4 $1 { with the threat of }  Bxg3 { , e.g. } 12. f4 gxf4 { 13.gxf4 Bd6 and Black should win - Baburin. } ) 
11. axb5 ( { Or } 11. bxa5 bxa4 12. a6 a3 $1 13. Nb4 ( 13. a7 { fails to } Bb6 ) 
Bb6 14. Kd3 Bg1 15. Kc4 Kd6 { , followed by ...Kc7, and the h-pawn soon decides - Baburin. } ) 
a4 12. Kd2 { White is just in time to control the a-pawn, but now his kingside is left undefended. } Kd7 13. c4 a3 14. Kc2 e4 $1 { The threat of ...Bxg3 seals White's fate. } 15. fxe4 fxe4 16. Nd4 Bxg3 17. Nb3 Bxh2 18. Nc5+ Kc8 19. Nxe4 Be5 
 0-1

