 { In this typical Grünfeld ending, White has several assets, such as the bishop pair, Black's badly placed knight and the passed d-pawn. But in order to make progress he needs something more to work on. } 1. { -KEY- } g5 $1 { Fixing a weakness on g6 which will reduce the mobility of Black's king. } ( { a) } 1. Bc8 $6 Nc4 2. Be6 Ke7 { was less productive. } ) 
 ( { b) But } 1. h5 $5 { also deserved attention, softening up the light squares for the king in similar fashion to Example 268 (Benko-Parma). } ) 
hxg5 2. hxg5 fxg5 ( { After } 2... f5 3. Be5 $1 Bd8 4. Bb8 { White wins - Krasenkow. } ) 
3. fxg5 Bb8 ( { Or } 3... Bd8 4. Kf4 { followed by 5 Be5. } ) 
4. Bc3 Bd6 5. Ke4 Ba3 6. Bd2 $1 { Not allowing even the slightest counterplay with 6...Bc1. } Ke7 7. Ke5 Bd6+ 8. Ke4 Ba3 9. Bf4 b5 { In view of the threat 10.Bb8, Black decides to sacrifice a pawn to activate his knight. } ( { The desirable } 9... Bd6 { failed to } 10. Bxd6 Kxd6 11. Kd4 b5 12. Bxb5 Nb7 13. Bd3 $1 { when White's first move makes itself remembered - Krasenkow. } ) 
10. Bxb5 Nb7 11. Kf3 $1 { Avoiding any forks and getting ready to defend the g5-pawn if necessary. } Nd6 12. Bd3 Kf7 13. Be3 a5 14. Bd2 $1 { One particular feature of the bishop pair is that the enemy pawns can be attacked on squares of either colour. } a4 ( { Or } 14... Bb4 15. Bxb4 axb4 16. Kf4 { etc. } ) 
15. Bc2 Nc4 16. Bf4 Nb6 17. Ke4 $1 ( { Avoiding the pawn exchange after } 17. d6 $6 Ke6 18. Bxg6 Bxd6 ) 
Bc5 18. Be5 Nc4 19. Bf6 Nd6+ 20. Kd3 a3 21. Kc3 $1 { Unfortunately for Black, his barrier can't be maintained due to zugzwang. } Be3 { Knight moves would let the king enter via c4 - Krasenkow. } 22. Kb4 { The rest is easy: White soon destroys the blockade on d6 by means of his king and dark-squared bishop. } Bc1 23. Kc5 Bf4 24. Bd3 Nc8 25. Kc6 Nd6 26. Bd8 Nf5 ( { a) Or } 26... Nc8 27. Kd7 Nd6 28. Bc7 { etc. } ) 
 ( { b) With } 26... Nf5 { Black sets the little trap } 27. Bc7 $2 Ne7 { but Illescas is on his guard. } ) 
27. Bxf5 $1 gxf5 28. d6 Bd2 29. d7 Kg6 30. Kd5 { Entering the square of the f-pawn before queening. } ( 30. Bf6 { (or } 30. Be7 { ) } Ba5 31. Kd5 $1 { (the similar } 31. Kc5 { and } 31. Kb5 { are also possible) amounts to the same thing, but not } 31. d8=Q $2 Bxd8 32. Bxd8 f4 { , when Black draws by sending the king to a8 in the end, on the topic of a rook pawn accompanied by the 'wrong' bishop. } ) 

 1-0

