 { Even in rook endings the idea of fixing pawns appears occasionally. Let's see two examples.Material is even, but Black's pieces are more active. With his next move, Kotronias opens a new front on the kingside. } 1... { -KEY- } h4 $1 { Such a majority attack is also quite common in the middlegame, and I touched upon the subject in 'Mastering Chess Strategy'. From now on, Black can either turn the opposing minority into a single pawn, or he can fix a weakness on h2 by a further ... h4-h3, which is what actually happened in the game. } 2. Rg1 ( { After } 2. gxh4 Rxh4 { the lonely h-pawn soon becomes a burden for White; e.g. } 3. Re1 Kd6 4. Re2 Kc5 $1 { and ...Rh3+ next. } ) 
h3 $1 { Fixing a weakness on h2 and creating a queening scenario. } ( { The alternative } 2... hxg3 3. hxg3 Rd4 4. Kc3 ( { or } 3... h3 3. Ke3 a4 $1 ) 
Rg4 { was interesting as well. } ) 
3. Rf1 g4 { Aware that White can't undertake much, Kotronias reinforces his pawn structure. } 4. b4 $2 { Overlooking a counterstrike on the queenside. } ( { Passive defence with } 4. Rf2 { was called for, when Black could choose between } Re1 { preparing ...Rg1-g2; } ( { and } 4. b4 f4 5. gxf4 Rxf4 6. Re2 Kf5 { in both cases keeping a clear initiative. } ) 
 ) 
axb4 5. axb4 b5 $1 { A strong blow that leaves White's pawn structure in ruins. } 6. cxb5 Rxb4 7. Rc1 Rb3+ $1 { A typical intermediate check, by which Black displaces the enemy king before landing his rook on b2. } 8. Kc4 Rb2 9. Kc5 Rxh2 10. Kc6 Re2 11. b6 ( { Equally hopeless is } 11. Kxc7 Kxd5 12. b6 Rb2 13. b7 f4 $1 { etc. } ) 
cxb6 12. d6 Rd2 13. Re1+ Kf6 14. d7 f4 $1 { This breakthrough decides the game. } 15. Rf1 Ke6 16. Re1+ Kf5 17. Rf1 f3 18. Rb1 ( { Or } 18. Kc7 h2 19. d8=Q $11 Rxd8 20. Kxd8 Ke4 { etc. } ) 
f2 19. Rb2 $5 f1=Q ( { In view of } 19... f1=Q $11 20. Rxd2 Qc1 { White resigned. } ) 

