 { Black is about to win a pawn, but first it makes sense to restrict the enemy pawns. } 1... { -KEY- } g5 $1 ( { A strong advance that gains some space and prevents White from playing h2-h4, establishing an ideal defensive structure; i.e. } 1... Rxb4 $6 2. h4 $1 { followed by 3.Rb6 with increased drawing chances. } ) 
2. f4 $5 ( { Alekseev opts for active defence; aware that after } 2. Rb6 Rxb4 { followed by ...f7-f6, Kg6-f5-e5 etc, Black has quite a favourable version of the typical rook ending with a passed queenside pawn, which we explored in the previous chapter. } ) 
g4 $1 { Fixing a weakness on h2. } ( { On the obvious } 2... gxf4 $6 { White had prepared } 3. Rb6 Rxb4 4. gxf4 { - Baburin, when the split kingside pawns makes it easier for him to create counterplay on that flank. } ) 
3. Rd6 Rxb4 4. Rd5 Rb2+ $1 { Cutting off White's king from the second rank. As we will see next, the bad location of this piece will play a crucial role in the remainder of the game. } 5. Kg1 b4 $1 { Nepomniachtchi ignores the threat at h5 and instead prepares the transfer of the king to the opposite flank. We already know from Chapter Three that, in such positions, it makes sense to advance the passed pawn to the third rank before activating the king. } ( { In contrast, the materialistic } 5... Kg6 6. Rg5 Kh6 $6 { leads nowhere after } 7. Rf5 ) 
6. Rxh5 b3 7. Rb5 Kf6 8. Kf1 { A committal choice, abandoning the kingside in order to fight against the b-pawn. } ( { By the way, if the f-pawn was still on f2 then } 8. Rb7 { would have made sense, but here this just invites } Kf5 9. Rxf7 Ke4 { with the dual threat of 10...Kf3 and 10...Rc2 followed by 11...b2. } ) 
 ( 8. h3 $5 { trying to create a passed pawn on the kingside is an interesting try, e.g. } gxh3 9. Kh1 $1 { - so that Black's rook can't shift to a better angle by ...Rg2+ followed by ...b3-b2- } Ke6 10. g4 $1 { as observed by ex_IM. Let's look a little further: } Kd6 { (as mentioned in the note to 5...b4, Black's natural plan here is a king transfer towards the b-pawn) } 11. g5 Kc6 12. Rb8 Kd5 13. f5 Kc4 { ( } Ke5 $5 14. g6 fxg6 15. fxg6 Kf6 16. Rb6 Kg7 { looks like a decisive zugzwang, however after } 17. Kg1 Rg2 18. Kh1 b2 19. Rb7 Kxg6 20. Rb6 $1 { the 'crazy rook' apparently saves the day, e.g. } Kf5 21. Rb5 Ke4 22. Rb4 Kd3 23. Rb3 Kc2 24. Rxb2 $1 { etc.) } 14. Rc8 Kd3 15. Rd8 $1 Ke4 16. g6 fxg6 17. fxg6 Rg2 18. Rb8 { when Black's king is one step too far away from the b-pawn. Anyway, we can't blame Alekseev for missing such subtleties at the board - I'd say that Black's position after 7...Kf6 could be regarded as almost decisive in practical play. By the way, the similar 8.h4 probably amounts to the same thing, since Black is well advised to reply 8...gxh3, considering that a passed pawn on the h-file would severely restrict their play. } ) 
Ke7 9. Ke1 Kd6 10. Kd1 Kc6 11. Rb8 Kd5 12. Kc1 Rc2+ 13. Kb1 ( { Or } 13. Kd1 Kc4 { and the king can hide from the checks on b2. } ) 
Rxh2 14. Rxb3 { White has again managed to restore the material balance, but now the weakness on g3 takes its toll. } Rf2 $1 15. Kc1 Rf3 16. Rb7 ( 16. Rxf3 gxf3 17. Kd2 Ke4 { produces a pawn endgame that had to be calculated in advance. After } 18. Ke1 Ke3 { ( } Kf5 $5 { heading for g4 might be even simpler, as observed by DolphinUnicorn, when Black always has a spare tempo with the f-pawn in the pocket) } 19. f5 f2 20. Kf1 Kf3 21. f6 Kxg3 { , the tempi again end up in Black's favour. } ) 
Ke6 ( 16... Rxg3 17. Rxf7 Ke4 18. f5 Ra3 19. f6 Ra6 20. Re7 Kf4 21. f7 Rf6 22. Kd2 g3 { is another winning line - Baburin. } ) 
17. Rb6+ Kf5 18. Rb5+ ( 18. Rb7 f6 { is useless, White has to help Black's king in improving itself. } ) 
Ke4 19. Re5+ Kd3 20. Rf5 Rxg3 21. Rxf7 Rf3 22. f5 g3 23. Rg7 Ke2 ( { In view of } 23... Ke2 24. f6 Kf2 25. f7 g2 { White resigned. } ) 

 0-1

