 { Here is a final example on fixing pawns, which shows that the piece favoured by such action can even be the king.White is slightly more active, but Black only needs a few tempi to achieve ... Kf6, ...Be6 and ...Rad8 with a complete mobilization of his forces. Polugaevsky came up with a clever idea. } 1. { -KEY- } a4 $1 Kf6 $6 { It seems natural to bring up the king, but it was more relevant to prevent White's queenside expansion. } ( { I suspect that during the rest of the game, Tal regretted not having played } 1... a5 $1 { at this moment, which also fixes the a-pawns on squares of the 'right' colour in relation to the bishops. } ) 
2. a5 $1 { Gaining more space and creating the positional threat of a5-a6. } a6 ( { a) Seeing that } 2... Be6 $6 3. a6 ) 
 ( { b) Or } 2... Rb8 3. a6 { leads to multiple weaknesses in his camp, Black settles for just one weak pawn on b7. } ) 
3. Kb2 { The beginning of an impressive plan: a king transfer to b6! } Be6 4. Kc3 $1 Rac8 $6 ( { Perhaps Tal played this in order to meet } 4... Rac8 4. Kb4 { by } c5 { but changed his mind on the next move, which reduces the text to the loss of a tempo. } ) 
 ( { The natural } 4... Rad8 { seemed okay; e.g. } 5. Rxd8 $5 ( 4... Rac8 4. Red3 Ke7 6. Kb4 Rxd3 7. Rxd3 f5 $1 ( { after } 7... Rd8 $6 8. Kc5 { the king reaches b6; } ) 
8. gxf5 Bxf5 9. Rd2 Rd8 10. Rxd8 Kxd8 11. c3 Kc7 { and Black holds the balance. } ) 
Rxd8 6. Kb4 { might be more critical, when Black should try } Bd5 ( 6... Rd2 $6 7. Be4 { leads nowhere; } ) 
7. Rd3 Ke7 ) 
5. Kb4 h5 ( { Black makes an attempt for counterplay; after seeing that } 5... c5 6. Kc3 { would leave the b7-pawn badly exposed; e.g. } Rc7 7. Rd6 Ree7 8. Rb6 { followed by Red3. } ) 
6. Bf3 $1 { A technical choice. White doesn't just keep his pawn structure intact, he also protects the e2-pawn in advance should Black manage ...Rd2 at some moment. } hxg4 7. hxg4 Rcd8 8. Rxd8 { Abandoning the d-file so as to hurry up the king's march to b6. } ( 8. Red3 $6 { was less promising in view of } Rxd3 9. Rxd3 e4 $1 10. Bxe4 Bxg4 ) 
Rxd8 9. Kc5 Rd4 { With the double idea of 10...Bxg4 and 10.Kb6 Rb4+. } ( { The passive } 9... Rd7 { could be met by } 10. Rd3 Re7 11. Rd6 Kg7 12. e4 $1 { followed by 13 Kb6, with Black completely deprived of counterplay. } ) 
10. c4 $1 { Simple and strong - Polugaevsky gives up the g4-pawn in order to secure the king's arrival at b6. } ( { a) } 10. Rd3 $6 Rf4 { was less convincing; } ) 
 ( { b) but } 10. c3 $5 { preparing b3-b4 and Kb6, also made sense. } ) 
Bxg4 11. Bxg4 Rxg4 12. Kb6 { Triumph for White's strategy, although there is still some work left to do. } Rf4 13. f3 ( 13. Kxb7 Rxf2 14. Kxa6 { was a more straightforward path to victory; e.g. } Kf5 15. Kb7 Kf4 16. a6 Kxe3 17. a7 { and the queen should prevail without much difficulty. } ) 
e4 $1 { Tal finds the only chance left for counterplay, but it won't be enough. } 14. Kxb7 Ke5 15. Kxa6 Kd4 16. Rxe4+ Rxe4 17. fxe4 g4 18. Kb7 { White will be a few pawns up in the queen ending, and the rest is a matter of technique for Polugaevsky. } g3 19. a6 g2 20. a7 g1=Q 21. a8=Q c5 22. Kb6 Kc3 23. Qd5 $1 { Centralization, a typical idea in queen endings in order to exploit the power of this piece to the maximum. } Qg8 24. Kxc5 Kxb3 25. Qd3+ Kb2 26. Qd6 $1 ( { With the strong threat of } 27. Kb6 ) 
Kb3 27. Qb6+ Kc2 28. Kb5 Qg4 ( { After } 28... Qe8 29. Qc6 Qb8 30. Ka6 { Black's checks come to an end. } ) 
29. Qd4 Qxe2 30. Kb6 Qh2 31. e5 $1 Kb3 ( { The black queen is denied the b8-square, and insisting with } 31... Qh8 { makes no sense after } 32. Qd6 ) 
32. c5 f6 33. c6 $1 { Simplest, exploiting the fact that Black's pawn on e5 will just obstruct his queen. } fxe5 34. Qd5+ Ka4 35. Qb5+ Ka3 36. c7 
 1-0

